WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.120
In Chimera, you can animate structure&nbsp;
translations and rotations in X-Y-Z,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.400
changes in the representation of ribbons and&nbsp;
atoms, changes in opacity and color, and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.760
In this tutorial, we will explore some of these&nbsp;
capabilities by creating a short animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.040
with the hemoglobin structure 4hhb.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.440
Before we begin to animate, it is important to&nbsp;
obtain the correct dimensions for the graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:34.160
window. If you're uploading to YouTube, you'll&nbsp;
want your dimensions to be of the ratio 1280 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:41.280
720. To set this ratio for the graphics window,&nbsp;
type into the command line: windowsize (all one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:50.320
word) space 1280 space 720 — and press Enter.&nbsp;
Now this is a relatively small window to work in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.760
so feel free to scale up the dimensions if&nbsp;
needed. For this tutorial, I'll be scaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:03.280
up by a factor of 1.5, which will make the&nbsp;
graphics window have dimensions of 1920 by 1080.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.320
Now we can go to Tools, Utilities,&nbsp;
and Animation to access the animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.000
interface. We will be using this interface&nbsp;
to create snapshot scenes called keyframes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.360
each containing a different depiction state of&nbsp;
the structure. Once we arrange these keyframes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.160
in the timeline, Chimera will transition&nbsp;
smoothly between them to create the animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.240
At this point, it's also worth noting that&nbsp;
Chimera's animation runs at a rate of 24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.840
frames per second. This value will become&nbsp;
an important conversion factor later on.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.400
To create our first keyframe, we'll take a&nbsp;
snapshot of the structure in its default state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.480
using the green "+" icon. Next, we're going&nbsp;
to set up a color change by going to Actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:01.040
Color, All Options, and selectively coloring&nbsp;
the ribbons green. Now we'll take a snapshot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.120
the result to create the second keyframe. It is&nbsp;
also possible to update keyframes once you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.480
created them. Let's say that you want to color&nbsp;
the protein ribbon something other than green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.040
To do this, we first make the desired change&nbsp;
and then right-click on the corresponding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:26.480
keyframe and select Update. Now that keyframe&nbsp;
contains the change made in the graphics window.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.080
For our final scene, we are going to zoom&nbsp;
into a heme group by first selecting chain B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:41.360
then changing the selection mode to&nbsp;
Intersect, and finally selecting Heme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.640
Then under Actions, we can focus in on&nbsp;
the heme group, clear the selection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.440
and take another snapshot to&nbsp;
create the final keyframe.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.680
It is now time to begin arranging the keyframes&nbsp;
in the timeline. To do this, we drag them in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:09.280
by one. Notice the number that appears at the&nbsp;
bottom as we drag them in — that number denotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.200
the number of frames currently contained in the&nbsp;
animation. Recalling that Chimera runs at a rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:22.000
of 24 frames per second, we see that the current&nbsp;
duration is about 3 seconds. By right-clicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.760
on the keyframe and selecting Properties,&nbsp;
we can set the transition length precisely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.600
This value denotes the number of frames&nbsp;
between the current and preceding keyframes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.200
Now the color change will take&nbsp;
exactly 3 seconds to complete.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.120
Similarly, we will allow 3&nbsp;
seconds for the zoom transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:51.920
by again going to Properties and then setting&nbsp;
the frame number to 72. In the timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.640
the three keyframes are equally spaced, in&nbsp;
accordance with our frame number settings.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.040
Finally, we can add a 360&nbsp;
degree rotation to the animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.280
by dragging the Roll action into the timeline.&nbsp;
We don't want the rotation to be too fast though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:15.440
so we will allow 7 seconds by going to&nbsp;
Properties and setting the frame number to 168.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.720
Now we are ready to export the animation. To do&nbsp;
this, we click the red and gray Record button.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.120
Now we can give our animation a&nbsp;
suitable name such as 4hhb-animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:40.240
Next we need to select the file format —&nbsp;
here, I'll go with the default H.264.mp4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.120
Finally, we hit record and wait&nbsp;
for the animation to render.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:55.840
With the export complete, we can&nbsp;
now view the finished animation.

